@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+---
+id: 0618
+title: A license notice the law requires ships verbatim with its author's name, in a LICENSE file beside the text it covers, and the no-names rule does not reach it
+status: accepted
+date: 2026-11-19
+tags: [license, mit, public-repo, names, skills, vendor, attribution]
+---
+
+# 0618: A license notice the law requires ships verbatim with its author's name, in a LICENSE file beside the text it covers, and the no-names rule does not reach it
+
+**What this decides:** text we adapt or ship from an outside project carries that project's license
+notice as written, even when its copyright line names a person.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  adapt["text adapted from an outside project"] --> license["its LICENSE, verbatim, in the same folder"]
+  license --> link["the text's attribution line links that LICENSE"]
+```
+
+```text
+.agents/skills/domain-modeling/
+  SKILL.md        ends: Adapted from <project> (MIT license, [notice](LICENSE))
+  LICENSE         the upstream MIT text, byte for byte
+```
+
+## Context
+
+[PR 1668](https://github.com/kamp-us/tea-fabrika/pull/1668) adapts three skills from an outside
+project under the MIT license. The MIT license asks that "the above copyright notice and this
+permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software", and a
+review judged the skills substantial portions. The notice's copyright line names its author, and
+AGENTS.md says "No personal names or account names". The owner ruled on 2026-11-19 that the
+notice ships, one LICENSE file in each skill folder. The plan for the anti-slop plugin
+(`docs/plan/quests/anti-slop-install-PLAN.md`, "Their `LICENSE` holds its author's name") read the
+rule the same way for `vendor/anti-slop/LICENSE`, under
+[0563](./0563-anti-slop-plugin-as-it-ships.md). This record makes that reading the rule.
+
+This changes AGENTS.md's "This repo is public" list, which now names the exception and links here.
+
+## Decision
+
+**A license notice the law requires ships verbatim, its author's name included, and the public
+repo's no-names rule does not reach it.**
+
+- The no-names rule is about our own people and accounts: what they type, who they are, where
+  their machines keep files.
+- The notice goes in a file named `LICENSE`, byte for byte as the outside project ships it, in the
+  folder of the text it covers. Adapted text in several folders gets one copy per folder.
+- The adapted text's attribution line names the project, with its link, and links that `LICENSE`.
+- An account name stays out of our own folder and file names. It may appear in the notice and in
+  the source link.
+
+**Binding constraints**
+
+- Never edit a license notice's text, and never drop one to pass the leak grep. A hit goes to the
+  lead, who owns the word list.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The three skills of PR 1668 each ship the upstream `LICENSE`, and their companion files link the
+  copy in their skill's folder.
+- An adapted text with no license file is a review finding.
+- Not decided here: what share of an outside text counts as substantial. A review judges it per
+  case, and when in doubt the notice ships.
